LEAD = {
    "headline": "YOUTUBE CARRIES 44.4 PERCENT OF EVERY ADVERTISING MINUTE AMERICANS SIT THROUGH ON STREAMING, AND ALL OF STREAMING IS STILL ONLY 22.3 PERCENT OF TELEVISION ADVERTISING MINUTES.  THE NEW NUMBER GOES UP WHEN A PLATFORM SIMPLY RUNS MORE ADS",
    "deck": "Comscore launched a monthly television report this morning built on a metric nobody was quoting last week, and by this time next month somebody will be quoting it at you in a planning meeting.  It is not watch time and it is not reach.  It is the number of advertising minutes a person actually sat through, which means it rises when a platform shows more adverts, and that makes the league table it produces a different thing from the one everybody will read it as.",
    "stamps": [
        ("COMSCORE · 21 OCT", "https://ir.comscore.com/news-releases/news-release-details/comscore-launches-new-monthly-report-highlighting-ad-exposure"),
        ("DIGIDAY · 21 OCT", "https://digiday.com/future-of-tv/future-of-tv-briefing-youtube-tubi-top-streamers-share-of-ad-exposure-time/"),
        ("THE WRAP · 10 SEP", "https://www.thewrap.com/media-platforms/streaming/youtube-viewership-july-2026/"),
        ("PPC LAND · NIELSEN", "https://ppc.land/nielsen-gauge/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "Start with the metric, because the whole item turns on it and the release does not explain it.  Comscore calls it Ad Exposure Time, and it means the minutes of advertising a person was actually exposed to, added up across broadcast, cable and streaming inside a broadcast month, then split by channel and by platform.  Digiday, which got the report first, describes it as a combination of viewing time and advertising load — how long people watch, multiplied by how many adverts they are shown while watching.  That is Digiday's wording and not Comscore's.  Comscore's own release never defines the metric anywhere, which is worth knowing before you repeat it.",
        "Now the August figures.  Across all of American television, <mark>broadcast delivered 43.2 percent of advertising minutes, cable 34.5 percent and streaming 22.3 percent</mark>.  Inside streaming, <mark>YouTube took 44.4 percent of the advertising minutes, Tubi 11.6 percent and Hulu 8.5 percent</mark>.  Digiday says the YouTube figure excludes YouTube TV, the pay television bundle, and again that is Digiday rather than the release.  Multiply the two shares together and YouTube is about a tenth of all the advertising minutes Americans sit through across broadcast, cable and streaming combined, which is the figure Digiday also prints as roughly 10 percent.  My own arithmetic puts it at 9.9 percent.",
        "Here is the comparison that makes the number mean something, and it comes from a different firm.  Nielsen's most recent American report, covering July and published on 10 September, put <mark>streaming at 49 percent of all television viewing time and YouTube at a record 14.2 percent</mark>.  Nielsen has not published an August edition.  So: about half of the time people spend watching television is streaming, and a bit over a fifth of the advertising minutes they sit through is.  Two companies, two months, two different things being counted — but the gap is far too wide to be a rounding problem.",
        "The mechanism is sitting in the definition.  If the metric is time multiplied by advertising load, then a platform climbs this table by running more adverts, and a platform with a light advertising load looks small on it no matter how much time people spend there.  A league table of advertising minutes endured is partly a league table of how heavily each service is loaded.  Which means the sentence that will get repeated — YouTube is 44 percent of streaming advertising — is wrong twice.  It is 44 percent of the advertising minutes people sat through on streaming, and streaming is a fifth of television's advertising minutes.",
        "The trend in the release is the stable part and it is more useful than the headline.  Streaming's share of advertising minutes was <mark>16.1 percent a year ago, held around 19 percent for five months, then 21.1 percent in June, 21.9 in July and 22.3 in August</mark> — a gain of 6.2 points over twelve months.  The top two have not moved all year: YouTube first and Tubi second every single month.  Third place has rotated — Amazon Prime Video in May on 7.4 percent, Netflix in June on 8.0, Peacock in July on 7.2, Hulu in August on 8.5.  So the structure is two fixed leaders and a monthly coin toss behind them, and anyone building a plan off third place is building it on noise.",
        "What is missing is the part to be careful about.  There is no panel size in the release, no household count, no methodology section and no definition of the central metric.  The company selling the number also sells the television currency it comes from, and launched this report as a monthly publication on the same morning.  Separately, from a different Comscore product, 130.6 million American adults used a free or advertising-supported streaming service in the three months to August, which is 47 percent of the adult population, and more than 74 percent of American households stream on a connected television.",
    ],
    "numbers": [
        ("44.4%", "of the advertising minutes people sat through on streaming in August went out on YouTube, against 11.6 percent on Tubi"),
        ("22.3%", "of all television advertising minutes came from streaming, against 43.2 percent broadcast and 34.5 percent cable"),
        ("49%", "of television viewing time was streaming in July on Nielsen's separate count — about half the watching, a fifth of the adverts"),
    ],
    "flagnote": "Comscore sells television measurement and launched The Score Report as a monthly publication on the same day it published these figures, so the study and the product are the same business.  The release discloses no panel size, no household counts and no methodology, and it does not define Ad Exposure Time anywhere — both the time-multiplied-by-advertising-load description and the exclusion of YouTube TV come from Digiday's reporting rather than Comscore's wording.  The Nielsen figure is a different company measuring a different thing in a different month, published on 10 September from July data, so treat 49 percent of viewing against 22.3 percent of advertising minutes as two measurements set side by side rather than one subtracted from the other.  The 130.6 million figure comes from a separate Comscore product on a three-month base.  Named in the release: Kevin Burns, Comscore's interim chief revenue officer, who told Digiday the company is the scorekeeper and not a commentator in the booth.",
    "so_what": "Somebody will walk into a meeting in the next fortnight and say YouTube is 44 percent of streaming advertising, and the correct answer is that it is 44 percent of a fifth.  The deeper reading is more useful than the correction.  If about half the television watching is streaming and only a fifth of the advertising minutes are, the shortfall is not a measurement error — it is viewing that currently carries very little advertising, because advertising load is a platform decision and these platforms have not made it yet.  You cannot buy your way into that gap, because the inventory is not for sale.",
    "do_this": "Write the one-line correction into your own deck language today, before a client builds a reallocation case on the headline: say advertising minutes sat through, never share of streaming advertising.  Then take the two numbers to your next owned-video conversation and use them in that order — half the watching, a fifth of the adverts — because the gap between them is the clearest argument available this week for making video rather than buying it.  And ask your measurement supplier which single metric they will defend in January, since Comscore has just made the case that viewing share is no longer enough on its own.",
}

